14:["$","section",null,{"className":"mb-20 prose prose-invert max-w-none border-y border-border/40 py-20","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-3xl mx-auto","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-display text-4xl font-bold mb-10 text-center","children":"The Biology of Stillness"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-xl leading-relaxed mb-8","children":"Modern neuroscience confirms what the Vedic masters taught thousands of years ago: the mind is an instrument tied to the breath (Prana). When the nervous system is in a high-beta state (stress/anxiety), the cognitive ability to \"just sit\" is essentially offline."}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-10 bg-muted/20 p-8 rounded-3xl border border-border/40 mb-10","children":[["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","h4",null,{"className":"text-orange-400 font-bold mb-2","children":"The Scientific View"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground","children":"Meditation activates the Anterior Cingulate Cortex (ACC) and the Prefrontal Cortex (PFC), while dampening the Amygdala. If the Amygdala is overactive, you must use breath or sound (mantra) to modulate it before cognitive stillness is possible."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","h4",null,{"className":"text-orange-400 font-bold mb-2","children":"The Vedic View"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground","children":"The mind (Manas) is like a mirror. If the dust of \"Rajas\" (activity/passion) is high, the mirror is obscured. You don't try to see the reflection; you first clean the dust using active practices like Kriya or Japa."}]]}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-lg text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed text-center italic","children":"\"You don't fight the mind to reach stillness; you negotiate with the body to allow it.\""}]]}]}]
